results: Multiple results

The results (A, B) of a function are a std::tuple<A, B>. return a, b is return {a, b};,
which evaluates left to right. q, r := f() stores the tuple in a temporary and reads each
element with std::get, and it works when one of the names already exists (a, err := ... with
an old err).

a, b = b, a evaluates the whole right side first, as Go says: it copies both values into
temporaries, then assigns. Named results are variables of the function; a function with
defer returns them through its single exit (gocpp explain defer).

Specification: docs/spec/cpp-mapping.md#multiple-results
See also: gocpp explain order, gocpp explain defer
Also known as: multiple-results, multiple results, tuple, tuples

Example (results.gp):

    package main

    import "fmt"

    func divmod(a, b int) (int, int) {
    	return a / b, a % b
    }

    func main() {
    	q, r := divmod(17, 5)
    	q, r = r, q
    	fmt.Println(q, r)
    }

The C++ that gocpp emit -annotate writes for it:

    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #pragma once
    #include <gocpp/rt.hpp>

    namespace gpkg_main {
    std::tuple<int64_t, int64_t> divmod(int64_t a, int64_t b);
    void main();

    } // namespace gpkg_main
    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/results.gp.cpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/results.gp.cpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #include "gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp"

    namespace gpkg_main {

    // ---- ./results.gp:5: func divmod(a, b int) (int, int) {
    std::tuple<int64_t, int64_t> divmod(int64_t a, int64_t b) {

    // ---- ./results.gp:6: return a / b, a % b
    // gp[checks] col 11: division of integers: gp::div panics for a zero divisor and gives Go's result
    //     for MIN / -1; these checks stay under -nochecks
    // gp[checks] col 18: remainder of integers: gp::rem panics for a zero divisor and gives Go's result
    //     for MIN / -1; these checks stay under -nochecks
        return {gp::div(a, b), gp::rem(a, b)};

    // ---- ./results.gp:7: }
    }

    // ---- ./results.gp:9: func main() {
    void main() {

    // ---- ./results.gp:10: q, r := divmod(17, 5)
        auto gp_t0 = divmod(17, 5);
        int64_t q = std::get<0>(gp_t0);
        int64_t r = std::get<1>(gp_t0);

    // ---- ./results.gp:11: q, r = r, q
        { auto gp_t1 = q; q = r; r = gp_t1; }

    // ---- ./results.gp:12: fmt.Println(q, r)
    // gp[interfaces] col 2: fmt.Println: the runtime's gp::fmt functions take each operand with the
    //     descriptor of its Go type (gp::fmt::as), not boxed into an interface first (an operand of a
    //     non-pointer type with an Error method is, see the allocation note)
        gp::fmt::Println(q, r);

    // ---- ./results.gp:13: }
    }

    } // namespace gpkg_main
